\section{The steps}{

pbn runs the steps in the order \code{raw}, \code{clean}, \code{cohort} and
\code{after_cohort}, and gives the value of each step to the next one. Each
step after \code{raw} MUST take exactly one argument without a default, or
\code{add_data()} stops. pbn calls \code{set.seed(sim)} once, before \code{raw}. So
\verb{raw = data_raw, clean = data_clean} draws the same numbers as
\code{fn = function(scenario, sim) data_clean(data_raw(scenario, sim))}.

pbn sees the value between the steps, so it checks the rule that the
cleaning drops no row. Each exclusion goes through the cohort, which counts
it. These values fail the data, as an error in \code{fn} does:
\itemize{
\item \code{raw} returns a data.frame, and \code{clean} returns a value that is not a
data.frame, or a data.frame with another number of rows.
\item \code{cohort} returns a value that is not a \code{cohort::CohortPipeline}.
\item \code{cohort} gets a data.frame, and its pipeline starts with another number
of rows, \code{cp$n_total()}.
}

The error gives both numbers of rows. The checks compare the numbers of
rows only, so the order of the rows and the columns MAY change. The checks
compare the number of rows, not which rows: a step that drops one row and
repeats another passes.

When \code{raw} returns a value that is not a data.frame, pbn does not check
\code{clean}. An example is a list of two tables that \code{clean} joins. That list
has no number of rows to compare, and it is a valid start. pbn does not
check the rows of \code{after_cohort}.

Use \code{fn} for data that does not fit the steps, for example data that
joins two cohorts. pbn then checks no rows inside \code{fn}.

An exclusion MAY apply to the rows of a reshape, for example one row for
each person and time point. Add these rows as a second data element, with
a second call of \code{add_data()}. Its \code{raw} builds the reshaped rows, and its
\code{cohort} applies the exclusion, so the cohort counts it. Its \code{raw} MAY
reshape the value of the \code{raw} of the first element. pbn sets the seed to
\code{sim} before each data element, so both draw the same numbers.
}

\section{A cohort as the data}{

\code{fn} or the step \code{cohort} MAY return a \code{cohort::CohortPipeline} after its
exclusions. The analyses then get \code{data[[name]]} as the data.table of
\verb{$get_included(<cohort>)}. \verb{<cohort>} is the one cohort of the pipeline
that no other cohort branches from. A pipeline with more than one such
cohort fails the data. Each other value goes to the analyses as it is.

The step \code{after_cohort} reads the data of the cohort with
\code{\link[=cohort_data]{cohort_data()}}, and returns its value. The value MAY be any R object
other than \code{NULL}, for example a list of imputed data sets. pbn puts the
exclusion table of the cohort and its number of rows at the start on the
value, as \code{\link[=keep_exclusions]{keep_exclusions()}} does. These replace the attributes that the
step set. So a call of \code{keep_exclusions()} in the step changes nothing,
and a table from another cohort does not stay. The step MAY return the
\code{cohort::CohortPipeline} that it got. Another \code{cohort::CohortPipeline}
fails the data, because its exclusion table would replace that of the
step \code{cohort}.

A step after the cohort in \code{fn} returns its value through
\code{\link[=keep_exclusions]{keep_exclusions()}}, which puts the exclusion table on it.

pbn keeps the exclusion table of the cohort, and \code{\link[=save_results]{save_results()}} stores
it in the element \code{exclusions} of the results. The table has one row for
each exclusion step of the cohort and of the cohorts above it, in the
order of the exclusion steps. It is a data.frame with these columns:
\itemize{
\item \code{data}, the name of the data.
\item \code{step}, the reason of \verb{$exclude_and_track()}.
\item \code{n_before}, \code{n_excluded} and \code{n_after}, the number of rows before the
step, excluded by the step, and after the step. \code{n_excluded} and
\code{n_after} are \code{n_excluded} and \code{n_remaining} of \verb{$consort()}.
}

The value carries the table in its attribute \code{pbn_exclusions}, and the
number of rows given to the pipeline in its attribute \code{pbn_n_before}.
\code{\link[=save_results]{save_results()}} writes that number to \code{manifest$n_rows_before_exclusions}.
The attributes are how both come back from a worker: the R6 object stays
on the worker.
}

\section{Cache}{

With \code{cache = TRUE}, \code{fn} MUST have the arguments \code{scenario} and \code{sim}
without a default. Call \code{\link[=initialize_project]{initialize_project()}} first. For
each scenario and simulation, \code{\link[=run_plan]{run_plan()}} writes the value to
\verb{<home>/<version>_cache_gitexclude/<name>/<scenario>/sim_<kkkk>.qs2}. The
value is the data.table and its exclusion table for a cohort. The cache
key goes to \verb{sim_<kkkk>_key.qs2} in the same folder. The cache key is the
md5 of these parts:
\itemize{
\item the scenario, the simulation and the settings of the scenario.
\item for \code{"real"} only, the fingerprint of the data at the time of the use.
It holds the name \verb{<folder name>/<path in the folder>}, the size and
the modification time of each file under each folder of \code{folders} of
\code{\link[=initialize_project]{initialize_project()}}. pbn reads no content of a data file. The path
of a folder is not in the key. So the same files in another place give
the same key when they keep their modification times. A copy with new
times looks like changed data, so the data is computed again. A file
that changed in place, and kept its size and its modification time, is
not seen.
\item the md5 of each file of \verb{<version>_functions/} whose name starts with
\code{data_}, for example \code{data_raw.R}, \code{data_clean.R}, \code{data_cohort.R} and
\code{data_impute.R}. \code{\link[=initialize_project]{initialize_project()}} computes them before it sources
the files.
\item the code of \code{fn}, or of each step, deparsed without its source
references.
\item \code{CFG$data}, when \code{CFG} has the element \code{data}. Put the config that the
data code reads there. A change in another element of \code{CFG} does not
change the key.
\item the versions of R and pbn. The version of R covers the base packages.
\item the three parts of \code{\link[=RNGkind]{RNGkind()}}.
}

The key file also records the version of each package that was loaded in
the R session that computed the value, after \code{fn} returned. On a worker,
that is the worker. The record leaves out the base packages. It also
leaves out the packages that run the plan, tests and documents: pbn,
plnr, foreach, doParallel, doFuture, future, future.apply, globals,
listenv, parallelly, progressr, iterators, qs2, digest, uuid, progress,
testthat, pkgload, devtools, callr, processx, ps, knitr, rmarkdown and
evaluate. A later use
compares each recorded version with \code{\link[utils:packageVersion]{utils::packageVersion()}} now. A
package with another version, or one that is not installed now, computes
the value again.

Each use of the cache stops when a \code{data_*.R} file on disk differs from
the file that \code{\link[=initialize_project]{initialize_project()}} hashed and sourced: changed, added or
removed. The error names the file. Run \code{\link[=initialize_project]{initialize_project()}} again. A
file with its recorded size and modification time is not hashed again,
so a change that keeps both is not found.

Keep the data code in the files \code{data_*.R}. The key holds no other file.
A function of the data code can be in another file. A change in it then
leaves the key the same, and the run reads the old data. This is the one
change of the data code that the cache does not find.

When the key is the same, \code{\link[=run_plan]{run_plan()}} reads the value from the cache and
does not call \code{fn} or a step. Each analysis sets its own seed, so its
results are the same as with a new value. Otherwise, or when a cache file
cannot be read, it calls \code{fn} or the steps and writes the cache. To compute every value again,
delete the folder \verb{<version>_cache_gitexclude/}. No argument does that.

For each scenario and simulation, \code{\link[=run_plan]{run_plan()}} prints one line with
\code{cat()}. The line says that the data came from the cache, with the date
and time of the cache file. Or it says that pbn computed it, and why.
The reason names each changed \code{data_*.R} file, \code{CFG$data}, or the
settings. It names changed raw data with its folders, for example "the
raw data in the folder lookup". It names a changed \code{fn} as "the data function of
add_data()", and each changed step as, for example, "the function given
as \code{clean}". It can also name the kind of random
number generator, or each package with another version. With \code{workers = 1}, the line comes before the
data function runs. With more workers, \code{\link[=run_plan]{run_plan()}} prints the lines after
the run, in the order of the plan.

\code{\link[=run_plan]{run_plan()}} writes each file to a temporary file in the same folder, and
renames it. plnr runs the data of one scenario and simulation in one task,
so two workers of one run never write the same file. When two runs write
one file at the same time, the last rename wins, and each file is whole.
The cache file holds its key too, so a cache file and a key file from two
runs give a new computation.

The cache folder holds data for each person. When the project is a git
repository, git MUST ignore it: \code{\link[=run_plan]{run_plan()}} stops before it runs anything
when git does not ignore the cache files. Add the line \verb{*gitexclude*} to
the \code{.gitignore} of the project.

\code{p$get_data()} and \code{p$run_one()} by hand use the cache too, and print the
same line. pbn computes the key at the time of the call, with the same
rules.
}

\examples{
p <- plnr::Plan$new()
pbn::add_data(
  p,
  "d",
  raw = function(scenario, sim) return(data.frame(X = stats::rnorm(3))),
  clean = function(d) return(stats::setNames(d, tolower(names(d))))
)
}
